## Doing the same work twice

The agent in Ch 23 re-sent the whole conversation on every call: 301 tokens, then 394, then 483.
Inside each call, the token loop from Ch 11 does the same: to pick each new token, the model reads
everything before it again. Does it really redo all that work every time?

It doesn't have to. Remember how attention works (Ch 9): every token makes a query, a key, and a
value. Each token can only look at the tokens before it, so its key and value depend only on the
text before it, and adding more text later doesn't change them. The model can work them out once
and keep them. This store is the <GlossaryPopover term="kv-cache">KV cache</GlossaryPopover>: with
it, each new token only computes its own key and value, and its query is matched against the stored
ones. Step through GPT-2 small finishing "The cat sat on the" with the cache off and on:

<KVCacheStepper />

Without the cache, every step runs the whole text through all 12 blocks again, so each step costs
a little more than the last. Measured on a laptop, the time per new token grew from 24 to 139
milliseconds over 500 tokens, and the whole run took 43.2 seconds. With the cache, each token took
about 20 milliseconds, and the run took 10.0 seconds. Both runs picked exactly the same 500 tokens;
their scores differed by at most 0.01, from rounding when the same sums are added up in a different
order.

The price is memory. GPT-2 small stores a key and a value in each of its 12 blocks for every token:
2 × 12 × 768 = 18,432 numbers per token. For large models and long prompts the cache runs to
gigabytes, and keeping it small is a big part of serving them efficiently.<Cite id="pope2022" /> The
recurrent models in Ch 30 avoid it by keeping a fixed-size memory instead.

## Caching across calls

The KV cache for one reply is thrown away when the reply is done. But an agent's next call starts
with exactly the same text, plus a little more. So model providers keep the cache for the start of
a prompt for a while, from minutes to hours, and a later prompt that starts the same way, token for
token, picks up from there. This is <GlossaryPopover term="prompt-caching">prompt caching</GlossaryPopover>.
Providers charge a tenth of the normal price or less for tokens read from the cache, and the reply
starts sooner, because there is less to work through first.<Cite id="openai-prompt-caching" /><Cite id="anthropic-prompt-caching" />

The match has to be exact, from the very first token. Change one early word and every key and value
after it is different, because each depends on all the text before it. So the cache only helps up
to the first difference. See what that means for the Ch 24 session:

<PromptCacheView />

- **Only appending** reuses 6,100 of the 7,656 tokens sent (80%). Each call only pays full price for
  what's new.
- **Putting the time at the top** of the system prompt makes every prompt different from its first
  line, so nothing is reused.
- **Editing the system prompt** throws the cache away once; later calls build a new one.
- **Dropping the oldest task** (Ch 24) sends fewer tokens, 5,776, but costs more than keeping them
  all: every drop changes the text right after the system prompt, so little else matches.
- **Summarizing** sends the fewest tokens and caches well between summaries, so it costs the least.

That is why harnesses keep the parts that don't change at the front, in the same order every time:
tool definitions, then the system prompt, then the conversation. Anything that changes, like the
time or the new message, goes at the end, and the conversation only grows. The providers' own
guides give the same advice.<Cite id="openai-prompt-caching" />

The cache is not memory. It holds the model's working for text that is still in the prompt, so the
model knows nothing more than it did (Ch 24), and it is deleted after a while without use. It
doesn't change the answer either: a cached prompt gets the same kind of reply as an uncached one.

Simplified: providers only cache prompts above a minimum length, from a few hundred to a few
thousand tokens depending on the model, and charge a little extra to write the cache. This small
session would be too short, so the view above ignores that limit.

<RecapCard>

- A token's keys and values depend only on the text before it, so the model can store them: the
  KV cache.
- With the cache, each new token costs about the same; without it, each costs more than the last.
  The answer is the same either way.
- Providers keep the cache for the exact start of a prompt, so harnesses put what doesn't change
  first and only ever append.

</RecapCard>

**Next question:** the harness fills the prompt with text from many places: tool results, web
pages, files. What if some of that text gives the model instructions of its own?
